INT. COURTHOUSE - JURY DELIBERATION ROOM - DAY

A bare table, twelve chairs, a ticking radiator. Five jurors remain at the table; the others are on break.

GRETCHEN LANDOWSKI (JUROR 3) (50s, perm, sharp elbows) taps her pen. MOHAN IYER (JUROR 5) (40s, tie loosened, calculator) stares at the table. BETTINA OYELOWO (JUROR 11) (60s, church hat) folds her hands. WALT BRZEZINSKI (JUROR 12) (70s, hearing aid, windbreaker) peels an orange. JUROR 8 (30s, rolled sleeves, tired eyes) stands by the window.

GRETCHEN
It's open and shut.

JUROR 8
Is it?

GRETCHEN
Ten to two. We've been here for six hours.

JUROR 8
I'm not asking you to change your mind. I'm asking you to hear mine.

MOHAN
We've heard it.

JUROR 8
Have you?

BETTINA
Let him speak, Mohan.

WALT
(too loudly)
What did he say?

BETTINA
He wants to speak!

WALT
Then let him!

Juror 8 turns from the window. He looks at each face.

JUROR 8
My name is Isaac. I know we've avoided names. But a man's life is on that table, and I don't want to decide it with numbers.

GRETCHEN
Fine. Isaac. Say your piece.

ISAAC
The witness couldn't have seen the face at that distance. I stood at the same window yesterday.

MOHAN
You went to the scene?

ISAAC
I went home. I stood at my own window.

GRETCHEN
That's not evidence.

ISAAC
Neither is a guess.

Silence. The radiator knocks. Walt offers Bettina half his orange. She takes it.
